arXiv:2509.08913v1 [eess.IV] 10 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Sin-Yu Huang and Vincent W.S. Wong
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>
Department of Electrical and Computer Engineering, The University of British Columbia, Vancouver, Canada
<br class="ltx_break">Email: {syhuang, vincentw}@ece.ubc.ca

</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Semantic communication has shown outstanding performance in preserving the overall source information in wireless transmission. For semantically rich content such as images, human users are often interested in specific regions
depending on their intent. Moreover, recent semantic coding models are mostly trained on specific datasets. However, real-world applications may involve images out of the distribution of training dataset, which makes generalization a crucial but largely unexplored problem. To incorporate user’s intent into semantic coding, in this paper, we propose a generalized user-oriented image semantic coding (UO-ISC) framework, where the user provides a text query indicating its intent. The transmitter extracts features from the source image which are relevant to the user’s query. The receiver reconstructs an image based on those features. To enhance the generalization ability, we integrate contrastive language image pre-training (CLIP) model, which is a pretrained large vision-language model (VLM), into our proposed UO-ISC framework. To evaluate the relevance between the reconstructed image and the user’s query, we introduce the user-intent relevance loss, which is computed by using a pretrained large VLM, large language-and-vision assistant (LLaVA) model. When performing zero-shot inference on unseen objects, simulation results show that the proposed UO-ISC framework outperforms the state-of-the-art query-aware image semantic coding in terms of the answer match rate.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The advent of intelligent applications, such as autonomous vehicles, augmented reality/virtual reality (AR/VR), and remote surgery, has driven the demand for higher data rates, lower latency, and higher reliability. To meet these requirements, the sixth-generation (6G) wireless networks are anticipated to shift from the traditional data-oriented communication to semantic communication <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. In semantic communication, the goal is to transmit information that preserves the meaning and relevance of the intended task <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>.
In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, Huang <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> introduced the rate-semantic-perceptual loss, which jointly evaluates the bit rate, semantic loss, and task-execution loss. However, the tasks considered in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, such as detection, segmentation and classification, are all machine-centric and do not fully reflect human-centric communication scenarios, where the intent is often complex and dynamic. For example, given an image of a horse on a grassy ranch with people nearby, a rancher may ask: “Where is my horse?”, focusing on the animal. On the other hand, the parents may ask: “Are my kids in the farm?”, prioritizing the people. This example illustrates the importance of semantic content depends on the user’s intent. Thus, semantic coding should adaptively prioritize the content relevant to the user’s intent. This capability is crucial for applications such as AR/VR and surveillance, where user’s intent may change depending on the scenarios.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, foundation models, such as ChatGPT and Gemini, have shown outstanding reasoning capabilities. Some studies (e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>) have leveraged foundation models in semantic coding. Other related works have integrated foundation models into semantic coding guided by the user’s query. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>, Chen <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">et al.</span> used a large language model (LLM) to generate answers and select key semantic elements from images based on user’s query. However, they did not consider the generalization ability on unseen objects. In real-world applications, the input data are diverse and may include previously unseen objects, making generalization a critical challenge for semantic coding. In our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, we explored the zero-shot capability of the semantic coding for text transmission. However, the generalization ability of image-based semantic coding remains largely unexplored.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08913v1/Model.jpg" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:467/181;" width="467" height="181" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The system model of the proposed generalized UO-ISC framework. As an example, we consider <math id="S1.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}=\{1,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}=\{1,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> in the transmitter.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we aim to answer the following questions:</p>
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Q1:</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">When transmitting an image with semantically rich content, how to incorporate user’s intent into semantic coding?</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">Q2:</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">How to improve the generalization capability of semantic coding?</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a generalized user-oriented image semantic coding (UO-ISC) framework, which prioritizes the semantic features relevant to user’s intent for wireless transmission. To incorporate the user’s intent, the transmitter takes the image and text query as input. To improve the generalization capability, we use contrastive language-image pre-training (CLIP) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which is a pretrained large vision-language model (VLM), to extract the features from the image and text query. These features are aligned, partitioned into segments, and quantized using a segment-wise codebook. The receiver of the UO-ISC framework reconstructs the image based on the received semantic features.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose a user-intent relevance loss function to evaluate the relevance between the generated image and the original image for a given text query. The loss is determined by large language-and-vision assistant (LLaVA) model. Moreover, we propose a two-phase optimization algorithm to jointly train the model using the user-intent relevance loss, <math id="S1.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> loss, quantization loss, and adversarial training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, aiming to enhance both the semantic relevance with the user’s query and the image quality through discrete feature representations.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For performance evaluation, we train our proposed UO-ISC framework on the visual question answering (VQA) dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> and perform zero-shot evaluation. When comparing the answers between the reconstructed and original images with the same query, results show that integrating CLIP improves the answer match rate by 34%. Furthermore, incorporating user intent provides an additional 5% improvement over the model without text query alignment and a 4.8% improvement over the state-of-the-art query-aware image semantic coding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This paper is organized as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="II System Model ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> presents the system model of the proposed UO-ISC framework. Section <a href="#S3" title="III Problem Formulation and the Proposed Two-phase Training Algorithm ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> introduces the user-intent relevance loss and two-phase training algorithm for UO-ISC. The performance evaluation is given in Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Performance Evaluation ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a>. Section <a href="#S5" title="V Conclusion ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> concludes the paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Notations:</span> We use boldface lowercase letters to denote vectors, boldface uppercase letters to denote matrices, and calligraphic letters to represent sets. For a matrix <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐗</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote its transpose as <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐗</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, its <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th row as vector <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(i,j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(i,j)</annotation></semantics></math>th element as <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[\mathbf{X}]_{i,j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝐗</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[\mathbf{X}]_{i,j}</annotation></semantics></math>. The expectation operator is denoted by <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\odot" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊙</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\odot</annotation></semantics></math> indicates element-wise multiplication. The <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm and <math id="S1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>-norm are denoted by <math id="S1.p5.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\|\cdot\|_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">∥</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\|\cdot\|_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The dot product of vectors <math id="S1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{a}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐚</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{a}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐛</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted by <math id="S1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><mi>𝐚</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐛</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}\rangle</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">System Model</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our proposed generalized UO-ISC framework is illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. It consists of a transmitter and a receiver. The transmitter obtains an image from sensing or stored files. Before transmission, the user provides a text query indicating the intended semantic content of the image to the transmitter based on prior knowledge of the scene. The transmitter then takes both the image and the text query as input. The goal is to reconstruct an image at the receiver while taking into account the semantic content relevant to the user’s intent.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, in the transmitter, we leverage CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, which is a large VLM pretrained on extensive image-text pairs, to extract the features from the text query and the input image. Let <math id="S2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input text query. Let <math id="S2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>H</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times H\times W}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the input image, where <math id="S2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>W</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W</annotation></semantics></math> are the number of channels, the dimensions along the height and width directions, respectively. The text query is processed by the CLIP text transformer to extract the text feature matrix <math id="S2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{T}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{T}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{T}}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{T}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of text embeddings and <math id="S2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension. The image is processed by the CLIP visual transformer, which consists of <math id="S2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> transformer layers. For each layer <math id="S2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}=\{1,2,\ldots,M\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}=\{1,2,\ldots,M\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the output feature matrix is denoted by <math id="S2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of image embeddings. Inspired by skip connections in U-Net <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we forward the features from different encoder layers to the decoder in order to preserve the spatial details for better reconstruction. However, transmitting the features from all transformer layers incurs a large transmission overhead. Thus, we only select a subset of transformer layers indexed by <math id="S2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}=\{m_{1},\dots,m_{M_{\text{S}}}\}\subseteq\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}=\{m_{1},\dots,m_{M_{\text{S}}}\}\subseteq\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{S}}=|\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}|\leq M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{S}}=|\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}|\leq M</annotation></semantics></math>. Only the feature matrices <math id="S2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="S2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, are processed in the subsequent transmission pipeline. Note that all transformer layers in the CLIP visual and text encoders are kept frozen to preserve the pretrained model’s generalization ability.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To extract the image features which are relevant to the user’s intent, we align the text and image features by projecting both modalities into a shared embedding space using a multilayer perceptron (MLP). The projected image and text feature matrices are given by:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{I}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{I}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{P}_{\text{I}}^{m}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}^{m};\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D},\quad m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{P}_{\text{I}}^{m}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}^{m};\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D},\quad m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{P}_{\text{T}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{T}};\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{T}}\times D}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{P}_{\text{T}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{T}};\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}})\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{T}}\times D},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\text{I}}^{m}(\cdot;\,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\text{I}}^{m}(\cdot;\,\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\text{T}}(\cdot;\,\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\text{T}}(\cdot;\,\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}})</annotation></semantics></math> are the projection functions for the <math id="S2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>th selected image features and text features with parameter sets <math id="S2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Each projected image feature matrix <math id="S2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{I}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{I}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is paired with the projected text feature matrix <math id="S2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>. They are processed by Feature-wise Linear Modulation (FiLM) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> to align the two modalities. Specifically, <math id="S2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> is projected through two MLPs to generate the scale matrix <math id="S2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sc}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sc}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> and shift matrix <math id="S2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sh}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sh}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sc}}^{m}=\mathcal{E}_{\text{sc}}^{m}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}};\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sc}}^{m}),~\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sh}}^{m}=\mathcal{E}_{\text{sh}}^{m}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}};\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sh}}^{m})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sc}}^{m}=\mathcal{E}_{\text{sc}}^{m}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}};\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sc}}^{m}),~\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sh}}^{m}=\mathcal{E}_{\text{sh}}^{m}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T}};\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sh}}^{m}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{sc}}^{m}(\cdot;\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sc}}^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{sc}}^{m}(\cdot;\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sc}}^{m})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}_{\text{sh}}^{m}(\cdot;\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sh}}^{m})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mtext>sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}_{\text{sh}}^{m}(\cdot;\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sh}}^{m})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the MLP with parameters <math id="S2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sc}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sc}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sh}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{E,sh}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. The aligned feature matrix <math id="S2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is given by:</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sc}}^{m}\odot\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{I}}^{m}+\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sh}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D},\quad m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sc}}^{m}\odot\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{I}}^{m}+\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{T,sh}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D},\quad m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08913v1/Transfer.jpg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:381/193;" width="381" height="193" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The segment-wise vector quantization process in UO-ISC.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To further compress the features, we apply vector quantization to convert each embedding vector in matrix <math id="S2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> into a sequence of discrete integers. We partition each embedding vector into multiple segments and quantize each segment independently using its own set of codewords. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ II System Model ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, for an embedding vector with dimension <math id="S2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, we partition it into <math id="S2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> segments, where each segment has length <math id="S2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{L}}=\frac{D}{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>D</mi><mi>L</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{L}}=\frac{D}{L}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume <math id="S2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> is divisible by <math id="S2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, so that the embedding vector can be partitioned into segments of equal length. The <math id="S2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>th codebook <math id="S2.p4.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{l}=\left\{\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{cw}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub></mrow></msup><mo>}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{l}=\left\{\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}\right\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{cw}}}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of a set of codewords <math id="S2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{cw}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{cw}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of codewords. For the aligned feature matrix <math id="S2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain the quantized feature matrix <math id="S2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> by segment-wise vector quantization function <math id="S2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}(\cdot;\mathcal{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>seg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}(\cdot;\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p4.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}=\{\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\,|\,i=1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}},\ l=1,\ldots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}=\{\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\,|\,i=1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}},\ l=1,\ldots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of all codebooks. We have</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m};\mathcal{C})\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{N_{\text{I}}\times L},m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>seg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m};\mathcal{C})\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{N_{\text{I}}\times L},m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.2" class="ltx_p">The segment-wise vector quantization partitions each row feature vector <math id="S2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{x}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{x}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times D}</annotation></semantics></math> in the aligned feature matrix <math id="S2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> into <math id="S2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> segments of length <math id="S2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{L}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{L}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and assigns each segment to its nearest codeword in the codebook <math id="S2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>. The operation of function <math id="S2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m};\mathcal{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>seg</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}(\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m};\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is as follows. For each row index <math id="S2.p4.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{I}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{I}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and segment index <math id="S2.p4.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}</annotation></semantics></math>, we extract the <math id="S2.p4.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>th segment from the <math id="S2.p4.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th row vector of <math id="S2.p4.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}=\big(" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}=\big(</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle[\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}]_{n,\,(l-1)L+1},\ [\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}]_{n,\,(l-1)L+2},\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle[\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}]_{n,\,(l-1)L+1},\ [\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}]_{n,\,(l-1)L+2},\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\dots,\ [\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}]_{n,\,(l-1)L+N_{\text{L}}}\big)\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\dots,\ [\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}]_{n,\,(l-1)L+N_{\text{L}}}\big)\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p4.3" class="ltx_p">We obtain the quantized matrix <math id="S2.p4.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each quantized index is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\left[\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\right]_{n,l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\left[\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\right]_{n,l}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\arg\min_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}}\left\|\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}-\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\right\|_{2}^{2}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\arg\min_{i\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}}\left\|\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}-\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\right\|_{2}^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad n\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{I}}\},\;l\in\left\{1,\ldots,L\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.447em">,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad n\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{I}}\},\;l\in\left\{1,\ldots,L\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">After quantization, the quantized feature matrices <math id="S2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{\bar{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are then flattened and concatenated into a single symbol vector <math id="S2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{M_{\text{S}}N_{\text{I}}L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{M_{\text{S}}N_{\text{I}}L}</annotation></semantics></math>. This vector is then passed through the physical layer, including channel coding and modulation. The resulting complex-valued transmit signal is denoted by <math id="S2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{s}_{\text{x}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\text{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mtext>x</mtext></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℂ</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{s}_{\text{x}}\in\mathbb{C}^{N_{\text{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\mathrm{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\mathrm{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of transmitted symbols after physical layer processing. Consider a flat fading channel model, the received signal is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{s}}_{\text{x}}=h\mathbf{s}_{\text{x}}+\mathbf{n}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mtext>x</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐬</mi><mtext>x</mtext></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>𝐧</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\hat{s}}_{\text{x}}=h\mathbf{s}_{\text{x}}+\mathbf{n},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> is the complex channel gain, and <math id="S2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathrm{S}}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐧</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐈</mi><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>S</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}\sim\mathcal{CN}(\mathbf{0},\sigma^{2}\mathbf{I}_{N_{\mathrm{S}}})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the additive white Gaussian noise with noise variance <math id="S2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">At the receiver side, the received signal <math id="S2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{s}}_{\mathrm{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>x</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{s}}_{\mathrm{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> is processed in the physical layer module, including demodulation and channel decoding, to determine the discrete symbol vector <math id="S2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\hat{s}}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{M_{\text{S}}N_{\text{I}}L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\hat{s}}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{M_{\text{S}}N_{\text{I}}L}</annotation></semantics></math>. The vector <math id="S2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{s}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐬</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{s}}</annotation></semantics></math> is then reshaped back into a collection of quantized feature matrices <math id="S2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where each matrix <math id="S2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{N_{\text{I}}\times L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}^{N_{\text{I}}\times L}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Then, the segment-wise reconstruction function <math id="S2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}^{-1}(\cdot;\,\mathcal{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mtext>seg</mtext><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Q}_{\text{seg}}^{-1}(\cdot;\,\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math> is applied to each quantized feature matrix <math id="S2.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. This function reverses the quantization process by mapping each quantized element into its corresponding codeword in the codebook. Let the element in the <math id="S2.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th row and <math id="S2.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>th column of <math id="S2.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\bar{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> be denoted by <math id="S2.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{y}_{n,l}^{m}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{y}_{n,l}^{m}\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reconstructed embedding matrix, denoted by <math id="S2.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\mathcal{Q}^{-1}(\bar{\mathbf{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m};\,\mathcal{C})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒬</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\mathcal{Q}^{-1}(\bar{\mathbf{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m};\,\mathcal{C})</annotation></semantics></math>, is given by</p>
<table id="S2.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{c}^{1}_{\bar{y}_{1,1}^{m}}&amp;\mathbf{c}^{2}_{\bar{y}_{1,2}^{m}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{c}^{L}_{\bar{y}_{1,L}^{m}}\\
\mathbf{c}^{1}_{\bar{y}_{2,1}^{m}}&amp;\mathbf{c}^{2}_{\bar{y}_{2,2}^{m}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{c}^{L}_{\bar{y}_{2,L}^{m}}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\mathbf{c}^{1}_{\bar{y}_{N_{\text{I}},1}^{m}}&amp;\mathbf{c}^{2}_{\bar{y}_{N_{\text{I}},2}^{m}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{c}^{L}_{\bar{y}_{N_{\text{I}},L}^{m}}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd></mtd><mtd><mi mathvariant="normal">⋱</mi></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>1</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mtd><mtd><mo>⋯</mo></mtd><mtd><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>L</mi></msubsup></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}=\begin{bmatrix}\mathbf{c}^{1}_{\bar{y}_{1,1}^{m}}&amp;\mathbf{c}^{2}_{\bar{y}_{1,2}^{m}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{c}^{L}_{\bar{y}_{1,L}^{m}}\\
\mathbf{c}^{1}_{\bar{y}_{2,1}^{m}}&amp;\mathbf{c}^{2}_{\bar{y}_{2,2}^{m}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{c}^{L}_{\bar{y}_{2,L}^{m}}\\
\vdots&amp;\vdots&amp;\ddots&amp;\vdots\\
\mathbf{c}^{1}_{\bar{y}_{N_{\text{I}},1}^{m}}&amp;\mathbf{c}^{2}_{\bar{y}_{N_{\text{I}},2}^{m}}&amp;\cdots&amp;\mathbf{c}^{L}_{\bar{y}_{N_{\text{I}},L}^{m}}\end{bmatrix}\in\mathbb{R}^{N_{\text{I}}\times D},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}^{l}_{\bar{y}_{n,l}^{m}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}^{l}_{\bar{y}_{n,l}^{m}}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the codeword associated with index <math id="S2.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{y}_{n,l}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>y</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{y}_{n,l}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> from the <math id="S2.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>th codebook <math id="S2.p6.m16" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{l}=\{\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{cw}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>L</mtext></msub></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{l}=\{\mathbf{c}_{i}^{l}\in\mathbb{R}^{1\times N_{\text{L}}}\}_{i=1}^{N_{\text{cw}}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">After obtaining the reconstructed embeddings <math id="S2.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}</annotation></semantics></math>, each matrix is reshaped into a spatial feature tensor <math id="S2.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tilde{H}\times\tilde{W}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>S</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tilde{H}\times\tilde{W}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}\tilde{W}=N_{\text{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}\tilde{W}=N_{\text{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>. These spatial feature tensors are then passed through a decoder composed of <math id="S2.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> convolution layers, indexed by <math id="S2.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathcal{N}=\{1,\ldots,N\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathcal{N}=\{1,\ldots,N\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Each convolutional layer is represented by function <math id="S2.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{n}(\cdot;\,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>D</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{n}(\cdot;\,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, parameterized by <math id="S2.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>D</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and produces an output <math id="S2.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tilde{H}_{n}\times\tilde{W}_{n}\times D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n}\in\mathbb{R}^{\tilde{H}_{n}\times\tilde{W}_{n}\times D}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the spatial dimensions <math id="S2.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{H}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{H}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{W}_{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{W}_{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are progressively increase for each layer. Each convolutional block consists of two standard convolution layers and a transposed convolution to perform spatial upsampling, where each standard convolution layer is followed by instance normalization and a leaky rectified linear unit (ReLU) activation. The number of output channels is equal to <math id="S2.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> for all layers. The decoder processes the embeddings in reverse order, starting from the reconstructed feature tensor of the final transformer layer’s output <math id="S2.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S2.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the feature tensor <math id="S2.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is passed directly to the first convolutional layer. For <math id="S2.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>, the output from the previous layer <math id="S2.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n-1}\vskip 6.0pt" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n-1}\vskip 6.0pt</annotation></semantics></math> is fused with the upsampled version of the corresponding reconstructed embedding, defined as <math id="S2.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{up}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}-n+1}}=\mathcal{U}_{n}(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}-n+1}};\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{U}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>up</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>U</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{up}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}-n+1}}=\mathcal{U}_{n}(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}-n+1}};\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{U}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.p7.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}_{n}(\cdot;\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{U}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>U</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}_{n}(\cdot;\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{U}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes a learnable upsampling module. This upsampled tensor is then fused with <math id="S2.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> via a feature fusion MLP, defined as <math id="S2.p7.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{fused}}^{n-1}=\mathcal{F}_{n}\left(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{up}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}-n+1}},\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n-1};\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{F}}^{n}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>fused</mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>up</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>F</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{fused}}^{n-1}=\mathcal{F}_{n}\left(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{up}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}-n+1}},\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n-1};\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{F}}^{n}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S2.p7.m22" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{n}(\cdot,\cdot;,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{F}}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>;</mo><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>F</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{n}(\cdot,\cdot;,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{F}}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> merges the aligned features and intermediate output. The tensor <math id="S2.p7.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{fused}}^{n-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>fused</mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{fused}}^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math> is then passed to the <math id="S2.p7.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th convolutional layer</p>
<table id="S2.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{D}_{n}\left(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}}};\,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n}\right),&amp;n=1,\\[4.30554pt]
\mathcal{D}_{n}\left(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{fused}}^{n-1};\,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n}\right),&amp;n\in\mathcal{N}\setminus\{1\}.\end{cases}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>S</mtext><msub><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></msub></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>D</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>fused</mtext><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>D</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>∖</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{n}=\begin{cases}\mathcal{D}_{n}\left(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{S}}^{m_{M_{\text{S}}}};\,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n}\right),&amp;n=1,\\[4.30554pt]
\mathcal{D}_{n}\left(\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}_{\text{fused}}^{n-1};\,\boldsymbol{\phi}_{\text{D}}^{n}\right),&amp;n\in\mathcal{N}\setminus\{1\}.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.p7.2" class="ltx_p">In our work, the number of convolution layers <math id="S2.p7.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> is equal to the number of selected indices <math id="S2.p7.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M_{\text{S}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>M</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M_{\text{S}}</annotation></semantics></math> so that each transmitted feature can combine with the convolution output. The final decoded embeddings <math id="S2.p7.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{Y}}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> pass through the final convolution head, generating the reconstructed image <math id="S2.p7.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that all the trainable parameters in the encoder is included in the parameter set <math id="S2.p7.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\theta}=\{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{E,sc}},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{E,sh}}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝜽</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, I</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>P, T</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sc</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝜽</mi><mtext>E,sh</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\theta}=\{\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{P, I}},\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{P, T}},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{E,sc}},\boldsymbol{\theta}^{m}_{\text{E,sh}}\}_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and all the trainable parameters in the decoder is included in the parameter set <math id="S2.p7.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\phi}=\{\boldsymbol{\phi}^{n}_{\text{U}},\boldsymbol{\phi}^{n}_{\text{F}},\boldsymbol{\phi}^{n}_{\text{D}}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>U</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>F</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi class="ltx_mathvariant_bold-italic" mathvariant="bold-italic">ϕ</mi><mtext>D</mtext><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\phi}=\{\boldsymbol{\phi}^{n}_{\text{U}},\boldsymbol{\phi}^{n}_{\text{F}},\boldsymbol{\phi}^{n}_{\text{D}}\}_{n\in\mathcal{N}}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Formulation and the Proposed Two-phase Training Algorithm</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The proposed UO-ISC framework reconstructs the source image while prioritizing the semantic features relevant to the user’s intent, where the intent is specified by a text query. To evaluate the alignment between the reconstructed image with the user’s intent, we define the <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">user-intent relevance loss</span>, which is determined using the pretrained LLaVA model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>. LLaVA is a large VLM that integrates a pretrained visual encoder and an LLM via a lightweight projection module. To assess semantic alignment, both the original image <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}</annotation></semantics></math> and the reconstructed image <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> are paired with the same text query <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> and processed separately through LLaVA. The images are processed through the visual encoder <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}_{\text{v}}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>v</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}_{\text{v}}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and projection matrix <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{P}_{\text{v}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>v</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{P}_{\text{v}}</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{X}}=\mathbf{P}_{\text{v}}\mathcal{F}_{\text{v}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>X</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>v</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>v</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{X}}=\mathbf{P}_{\text{v}}\mathcal{F}_{\text{v}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{Y}}=\mathbf{P}_{\text{v}}\mathcal{F}_{\text{v}}(\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>Y</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐏</mi><mtext>v</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mtext>v</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{Y}}=\mathbf{P}_{\text{v}}\mathcal{F}_{\text{v}}(\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>.
The text query is tokenized into <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and concatenated with the image embeddings to form the multimodal sequences <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{X}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{H}_{\text{X}},\mathbf{H}_{\text{T}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>X</mtext><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>X</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{X}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{H}_{\text{X}},\mathbf{H}_{\text{T}}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{H}_{\text{Y}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{H}_{\text{Y}},\mathbf{H}_{\text{T}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>Y</mtext><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>Y</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐇</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{H}_{\text{Y}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{H}_{\text{Y}},\mathbf{H}_{\text{T}}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which are input to the language model. The output hidden states are denoted by <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{X}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{X}},\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{T}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>X</mtext><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>X</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{X}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{X}},\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{T}}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{Y}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{Y}},\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{T}}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>Y</mtext><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>Y</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>T</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{Y}}^{\text{M}}=[\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{Y}},\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{T}}]</annotation></semantics></math>. The user-intent relevance loss is defined as the cosine distance between the corresponding image tokens:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{user}}(\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})=1-\frac{1}{N_{\text{i}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{i}}}\frac{\langle\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{X},i}^{\text{M}},\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{Y},i}^{\text{M}}\rangle}{\|\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{X},i}^{\text{M}}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{Y},i}^{\text{M}}\|_{2}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>X</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>X</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{user}}(\mathbf{Y},\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})=1-\frac{1}{N_{\text{i}}}\sum_{i=1}^{N_{\text{i}}}\frac{\langle\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{X},i}^{\text{M}},\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{Y},i}^{\text{M}}\rangle}{\|\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{X},i}^{\text{M}}\|_{2}\|\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{Y},i}^{\text{M}}\|_{2}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{X},i}^{\text{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>X</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{X},i}^{\text{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{Y},i}^{\text{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐡</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mtext>Y</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{h}}_{\text{Y},i}^{\text{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>th token embedding vector from <math id="S3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{X}}^{\text{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>X</mtext><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{X}}^{\text{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{Y}}^{\text{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐇</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>Y</mtext><mtext>M</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{H}}_{\text{Y}}^{\text{M}}</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, and <math id="S3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>i</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the total number of token embedding vectors.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to user-intent relevance, the visual quality of the reconstructed image is also important for image semantic coding. To improve the visual quality, we incorporate the <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> loss and adversarial loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> into the training objective of UO-ISC. The <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ell_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ell_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> loss, defined as <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{1}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y})=\|\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}-\mathbf{Y}\|_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>1</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>𝐘</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{1}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y})=\|\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}-\mathbf{Y}\|_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, improves the pixel-level fidelity between the source and reconstructed images, and preserves low-level visual details such as edges and structural content. The adversarial loss improves the perceptual realism of the reconstructed images. In adversarial training, a lightweight discriminator is used to distinguish real images from the generated ones. The generator, which is our model, is trained to produce outputs that are indistinguishable from real images. Additionally, the segment-wise codebook <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> introduces a quantization loss. Let <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>cw</mtext></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q\in\{1,\ldots,N_{\text{cw}}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the index of the closest codeword obtained from (<a href="#S2.E7" title="In II System Model ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) and <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{c}_{q}^{l}\in\mathcal{C}_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{c}_{q}^{l}\in\mathcal{C}_{l}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the nearest codeword in the <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>th segment. Recall from (<a href="#S2.E6" title="In II System Model ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) that <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>th segment of the <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th row vector in <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐗</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mtext>align</mtext><mi>m</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\tilde{X}}_{\text{align}}^{m}</annotation></semantics></math>. The quantization loss is defined as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{quant}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{quant}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}\sum_{n=1}^{N_{\text{I}}}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\Big(\left\|\text{sg}[\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}]-\mathbf{c}_{q}^{l}\right\|_{2}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mtext>S</mtext></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>L</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{m\in\mathcal{M}_{\text{S}}}\sum_{n=1}^{N_{\text{I}}}\sum_{l=1}^{L}\Big(\left\|\text{sg}[\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}]-\mathbf{c}_{q}^{l}\right\|_{2}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hskip 50.00008pt+\beta\left\|\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}-\text{sg}[\mathbf{c}_{q}^{l}]\right\|_{2}^{2}\Big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mo>−</mo><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐜</mi><mi>q</mi><mi>l</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hskip 50.00008pt+\beta\left\|\mathbf{u}_{n,l}^{m}-\text{sg}[\mathbf{c}_{q}^{l}]\right\|_{2}^{2}\Big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{sg}[\cdot]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>sg</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{sg}[\cdot]</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the stop-gradient operator and <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a tunable parameter. Since the codeword selection function in (<a href="#S2.E5" title="In II System Model ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) is non-differentiable, the stop-gradient operator ensures separate updates to the encoder and codebook during backpropagation.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.08913v1/visualization_update.jpg" id="S3.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/99;" width="476" height="99" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S3.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization of the reconstructed images produced by the proposed UO-ISC and baseline methods with the SNR = 0 dB. The text query used for evaluation is: “Does the cat have a collar?”.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since jointly optimizing all loss terms may lead to unstable training, we adopt a two-phase training strategy. In Phase I, the adversarial loss is not considered, and the model is optimized using the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{phase-I}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})=\ " display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>phase-I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.778em">=</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{phase-I}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})=\ </annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y})+\lambda_{\text{user}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{user}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{1}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y})+\lambda_{\text{user}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{user}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle+\lambda_{\text{quant}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{quant}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle+\lambda_{\text{quant}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{quant}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{user}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>user</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{user}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{quant}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>quant</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{quant}}</annotation></semantics></math> are tunable parameters. This phase enables the model to learn semantic reconstruction from discrete features, even though the resulting images may lack visual sharpness. In Phase II, we introduce adversarial training to enhance perceptual realism. A lightweight discriminator <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\cdot\,;\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\cdot\,;\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}})</annotation></semantics></math> with parameter set <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to distinguish real images from the generated images. The adversarial loss function for the generator is defined as <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{gen}}(\mathbf{Y})=\mathbb{E}[\log\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{Y};\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{gen}}(\mathbf{Y})=\mathbb{E}[\log\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{Y};\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}})]</annotation></semantics></math>, which enables the generator to produce images to be indistinguishable from real samples. The overall loss function for Phase II is as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{phase-II}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>phase-II</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{phase-II}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{phase-I}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>phase-I</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mtext>T</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{phase-I}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y};\,\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\lambda_{\text{gen}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{gen}}(\mathbf{Y})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\lambda_{\text{gen}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{gen}}(\mathbf{Y}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>λ</mi><mtext>gen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a tunable parameter. After updating the generator, the discriminator is trained using the following loss function:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}},\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}[\log\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}};\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}[\log\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}};\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\quad-\mathbb{E}[\log(1-\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{Y};\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}}))]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝝍</mi><mtext>dis</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad-\mathbb{E}[\log(1-\mathcal{D}_{\text{dis}}(\mathbf{Y};\boldsymbol{\psi}_{\text{dis}}))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.4" class="ltx_p">where the first term encourages high confidence for real images <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐗</mi><mtext>I</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the second term penalizes for assigning high confidence to the reconstructed output <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐘</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>.
In Phase II, the training process alternates between our model and the discriminator. First, we update our model and keep the discriminator to be fixed. Then, we update the discriminator while keeping our model to be fixed. The complete training procedure is summarized in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ III Problem Formulation and the Proposed Two-phase Training Algorithm ‣ Generalized User-Oriented Image Semantic Coding Empowered by Large Vision-Language Model" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Proposed Two-phase Training Algorithm</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.5" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
            
          <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Input:</span><span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> Image data and query in the training datasets; number of epochs for the first and second phases, </span><math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{2}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">; learning rate for our model and the discriminator </span><math id="alg1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\text{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">gen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\text{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="alg1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta_{\text{dis}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">η</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">dis</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta_{\text{dis}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">; regularization weights </span><math id="alg1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{user}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">user</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{user}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{gen}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">gen</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{gen}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="alg1.l1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda_{\text{quant}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">λ</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">quant</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda_{\text{quant}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="alg1.l1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
            
          <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Phase I:</span><span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
            
          <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Freeze the parameters of pretrained CLIP in the encoder.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
            
          <span id="alg1.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">for</span><span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\leftarrow 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">i</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\leftarrow 1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">to</span><span id="alg1.l4.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="alg1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">N</mi><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{1}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l4.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="alg1.l4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">do</span><span id="alg1.l4.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
            
          <span id="alg1.l5.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  Load input image </span><math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐗</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">I</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{X}_{\text{I}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and query </span><math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐱</mi><mtext mathsize="0.900em">T</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{\text{T}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> from the dataset.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
            
          <span id="alg1.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  Sample the channel gain coefficient </span><math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and noise </span><math id="alg1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">𝐧</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">.
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
            
